{
  "uid": "skp-runtime",
  "title": "SKP Runtime Instrumentation",
  "description": "Runtime instrumentation across all four SKP service classes (webapi / orchestrator / keeper / processor). Metrics come from the process_runtime_dotnet_* family emitted by OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Runtime 1.15.0 on net8.0 (research F-1). A future net9.0 upgrade would rename the whole family to dotnet_* and blank every panel here; scripts/phase-87-dashboard-lint.ps1 rule S8 hard-fails on any dotnet_* reference so that regression surfaces hermetically in ~5s instead of as a silently empty dashboard.",
  "tags": [
    "skp",
    "runtime"
  ],
  "timezone": "browser",
  "editable": false,
  "graphTooltip": 1,
  "schemaVersion": 39,
  "version": 1,
  "refresh": "30s",
  "time": {
    "from": "now-1h",
    "to": "now"
  },
  "annotations": {
    "list": []
  },
  "links": [],
  "templating": {
    "list": [
      {
        "type": "datasource",
        "name": "datasource",
        "label": "Datasource",
        "query": "prometheus",
        "current": {},
        "refresh": 1,
        "regex": "",
        "hide": 0,
        "skipUrlSync": false
      },
      {
        "type": "query",
        "name": "source",
        "label": "Service class",
        "datasource": {
          "type": "prometheus",
          "uid": "${datasource}"
        },
        "query": "label_values(process_runtime_dotnet_gc_collections_count_total, source)",
        "definition": "label_values(process_runtime_dotnet_gc_collections_count_total, source)",
        "refresh": 2,
        "sort": 1,
        "multi": true,
        "includeAll": true,
        "allValue": null,
        "current": {},
        "options": [],
        "hide": 0,
        "skipUrlSync": false
      },
      {
        "type": "query",
        "name": "pod",
        "label": "Pod",
        "datasource": {
          "type": "prometheus",
          "uid": "${datasource}"
        },
        "query": "label_values(process_runtime_dotnet_gc_collections_count_total{source=~\"$source\"}, service_instance_id)",
        "definition": "label_values(process_runtime_dotnet_gc_collections_count_total{source=~\"$source\"}, service_instance_id)",
        "refresh": 2,
        "sort": 1,
        "multi": true,
        "includeAll": true,
        "allValue": null,
        "current": {},
        "options": [],
        "hide": 0,
        "skipUrlSync": false
      }
    ]
  },
  "panels": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "GC collections / sec by generation",
      "description": "How often .NET reclaims memory, split by generation - gen0 is cheap and frequent, gen2 is expensive and rare. Typical on this stack: under 0.02 collections/sec per class, nearly all of it gen0. Investigate when the gen2 line climbs toward the gen0 line, or the total holds above roughly 1/sec: objects are surviving long enough to be promoted, which shows up next as rising heap size and longer pauses.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "ops"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id, generation) (rate(process_runtime_dotnet_gc_collections_count_total{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}} / {{generation}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "GC heap size by generation",
      "description": "Managed memory currently held, split by generation. Typical per pod: 14-20 MB for orchestrator, keeper and processor. The WebApi is far more volatile and has been seen at 140-210 MB. Investigate when a pod floor climbs steadily across hours and never drops back after a collection - that is the leak signature. A high but flat line is a warm cache, not a problem.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 0
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "bytes"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id, generation) (process_runtime_dotnet_gc_heap_size_bytes{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}} / {{generation}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "GC heap fragmentation by generation",
      "description": "Wasted space inside the managed heap: memory held but not usable. Typical per pod: under 1 MB for keeper and processor, about 3.5 MB for orchestrator, with peaks near 9 MB. Investigate when fragmentation approaches a third of the total heap size shown on the panel to the left, or sits above roughly 20 MB. The process is then holding memory it cannot use, and gen2 collections get more expensive.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 8
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "bytes"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id, generation) (process_runtime_dotnet_gc_heap_fragmentation_size_bytes{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}} / {{generation}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Allocation rate",
      "description": "How fast each service class creates new objects. Typical: orchestrator about 100 kB/s, processor about 50 kB/s, keeper about 10 kB/s, WebApi near zero when idle. Investigate at roughly 10x a class usual figure sustained for minutes. Allocation is the upstream cause of most GC pressure, so a jump here normally explains a spike on the GC panels rather than being a separate problem.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 8
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "Bps"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id) (rate(process_runtime_dotnet_gc_allocations_size_bytes_total{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "GC committed memory",
      "description": "Memory the .NET runtime has reserved from the operating system, per pod - the closest thing this stack has to a memory-usage readout. It is NOT the OS working set: no OS-level memory metric exists here at all, and adding one would need either a code change or a new Prometheus scrape job, both out of scope for this milestone. Typical: 28-35 MB for orchestrator, keeper and processor; 80-230 MB for the WebApi. Investigate on a steady climb that never falls back, or a pod approaching its 512 MiB container limit. The WebApi reports nothing here until it performs its first collection, so an idle WebApi is legitimately blank.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 16
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "bytes"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id) (process_runtime_dotnet_gc_committed_memory_size_bytes{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Live object bytes",
      "description": "Bytes of objects that survived the last collection - the real in-use figure, as opposed to total heap. Typical per pod: keeper about 20 MB, processor about 23 MB, orchestrator about 25 MB, WebApi about 46 MB. Investigate on sustained growth with no plateau. This is the most trustworthy leak indicator on the dashboard: total heap can grow harmlessly, but surviving objects growing without bound cannot.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 16
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "bytes"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id) (process_runtime_dotnet_gc_objects_size_bytes{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "GC time per second",
      "description": "How much of each second the process spends collecting garbage - a stand-in for CPU load, which this stack does not measure. Read it as a fraction: 0.001 means 0.1 percent of the time. Typical here: under 0.0002, effectively idle. Investigate above roughly 0.05 (5 percent) and treat above 0.1 (10 percent) as urgent, because GC pauses are then eating real throughput. Deliberately NOT titled CPU: it is GC pressure, not processor time, and a panel labelled CPU that cannot measure CPU would be worse than no panel.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 24
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percentunit"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id) (rate(process_runtime_dotnet_gc_duration_nanoseconds_total{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"}[$__rate_interval])) / 1e9",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Thread-pool threads",
      "description": "Worker threads .NET currently has running, per pod. Typical: keeper about 4, processor about 10, orchestrator about 16, WebApi about 7. Investigate a steady climb that never settles. The pool grows when work blocks, so a rising thread count alongside flat throughput on the completion-rate panel points at blocked I/O or lock waits rather than genuine load.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 24
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "max by (source, service_instance_id) (process_runtime_dotnet_thread_pool_threads_count{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Thread-pool queue length",
      "description": "Work items waiting for a free thread, per pod. Typical: 0 - the pool keeps up everywhere on this stack. Investigate when any pod stays above 0 for more than about a minute, or trends upward: work is arriving faster than it can be run, and downstream latency follows. A brief spike during a burst is normal and needs no action.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 32
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "max by (source, service_instance_id) (process_runtime_dotnet_thread_pool_queue_length{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Thread-pool completion rate",
      "description": "Work items finished per second - the closest thing to a pulse for each service class. Typical: orchestrator about 110/s, processor about 58/s, keeper about 12/s, WebApi about 4/s. Investigate when a class falls toward 0 while the pipeline is still being fed, which is a stall and will also show as a widening gap on the business dashboard conservation panels. A drop while the stack is genuinely idle is expected.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 32
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "ops"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id) (rate(process_runtime_dotnet_thread_pool_completed_items_count_total{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Exception rate",
      "description": "Exceptions thrown per second, per class - thrown, not necessarily unhandled or fatal. Typical: 0 across every class. Investigate any sustained non-zero reading. A brief burst at startup or during a deliberate fault test is expected, but a steady trickle usually means a retry loop is failing repeatedly and swallowing the error, which no other panel on this dashboard will reveal.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 40
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "ops"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id) (rate(process_runtime_dotnet_exceptions_count_total{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 12,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Lock contention rate",
      "description": "How often threads collide waiting for the same lock, per second. Typical: under 1/s everywhere - processor about 0.6, orchestrator about 0.45, keeper about 0.16. Investigate above roughly 10/s sustained, particularly alongside a rising thread-pool queue length. Together those two mean threads are spending their time waiting on each other instead of doing work.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 40
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "ops"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "sum by (source, service_instance_id) (rate(process_runtime_dotnet_monitor_lock_contention_count_total{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 13,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Loaded assemblies",
      "description": "How many .NET assemblies each pod has loaded. This should be flat after startup. Typical: keeper 123, processor 126, orchestrator 131, WebApi about 200-210. Investigate any steady climb: assemblies are never unloaded, so a rising count is a genuine unbounded leak, normally caused by dynamic code generation or repeated reflection-based loading.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 48
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "max by (source, service_instance_id) (process_runtime_dotnet_assemblies_count{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 14,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Active timers",
      "description": "Timers currently scheduled in each pod. Typical: keeper about 6, processor about 7, WebApi about 8, orchestrator about 15, with WebApi peaks near 66. Investigate a count that only ever rises - that is timers created and never disposed, leaking both memory and wake-ups. A count that rises with load and falls back afterwards is healthy.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 48
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "range": true,
          "expr": "max by (source, service_instance_id) (process_runtime_dotnet_timer_count{source=~\"$source\", service_instance_id=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{source}} / {{service_instance_id}}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 15,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "JIT methods compiled / sec",
      "description": "Methods being compiled to machine code, per second. Expect a burst in a pod first minute of life, where peaks of 80-250/s are normal, then effectively 0. Typical steady state: under 0.02/s. Investigate sustained non-zero long after startup: code is still being generated at runtime, which costs CPU on every call and points at dynamic proxies or repeated reflection emit.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 56
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "ops"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
